## Goal

Stop technically valid but mechanically weak, concept-drifting, confusing, or subtly broken games from advancing. Turn browser-qualified prototypes into evidence-driven parent/child lineages that can repair and improve themselves.

## Why this is required

Live generations now expose several distinct failure classes:

- `Twin Mane Braid`: byte-valid/runnable but semantically wrong cleanup/motion/control logic.
- `Tetherfall Duet`: visible browser output plus source defects, frame-dependent physics, early-death behavior, and real cross-browser divergence. The original M3 sampler also falsely called visible Chromium/Firefox canvases blank.
- `Tether Twins` / run `33561501229`: the concept tournament qualified, but both implementation races died at **builder transport** before gameplay existed (Kimi timeout; Nemotron malformed JSON). This is infrastructure evidence, not a gameplay rejection.

The pipeline therefore needs explicit evidence layers. Browser green is not gameplay green, and an upstream infrastructure failure must never masquerade as a bad game.

## Phase A — source-aware gameplay critics: implemented and hardened

`game-engine source-audit` inspects the intended design plus generated HTML and Browser Reality evidence.

Dimensions include:
- concept fidelity;
- logic correctness;
- first-use clarity;
- game feel/controls;
- mastery;
- pacing/progression;
- visual/audio identity;
- replayability;
- exploit resistance.

Findings require severity, concrete evidence, player consequence, and smallest coherent fix.

Current hardening:
- generated source is explicitly **untrusted data**, never critic instructions;
- source is delimited in the critic prompt;
- when Browser Reality qualification exists, expensive critics now run only on `full_pass_build_ids`;
- a well-supported `repair`/`reject` remains successful evidence rather than workflow infrastructure failure.

### Current limitation

Critics remain uncalibrated. The current `overall +0.25` repair improvement threshold is not grounded in measured inter-rater variance, and critic families overlap with author/repair families. Behavioral evidence must dominate these prose scores once Phase B exists.

## Phase B — GameSpec-backed agentic play: next critical implementation

The current head now gives us a concrete contract to instrument: every prototype race receives a `GameSpec` defining primary category, interaction invariant, first-slice scope, timing requirements, state bounds, reset semantics, sensory mappings, and telemetry fields/events.

Phase B should verify those claims behaviorally rather than invent a separate generic telemetry ontology.

### Development telemetry interface

During unminified/evidence builds, expose a versioned read-only interface such as:

`window.__GAME_ENGINE_TELEMETRY__`

The game may report its state, but **self-reported telemetry is a claim, not ground truth**. M3/browser observations must cross-check it where possible.

Minimum snapshot aligned to current GameSpec:
- schema/spec version;
- source concept/spec/build ID;
- elapsed milliseconds / simulation tick;
- state: playing/dead/won;
- alive/game-over;
- score and normalized progress where defined;
- restart count;
- entity/object count;
- action count and last accepted-action time;
- core-mechanic activation count;
- progression-transition count;
- deterministic/compact state hash.

Minimum event stream:
- `run_start`;
- `action_accepted`;
- `progress_change`;
- `damage_or_death`;
- `win`;
- `restart`;
- `core_mechanic_activation`;
- `progression_transition`.

The interface is development evidence and can be stripped before final 13KB packaging unless its runtime value earns the bytes.

### Policy A — null baseline

No input.

Measure:
- idle survival time;
- idle score/progress;
- autonomous mechanic activations;
- entity growth;
- whether the game effectively plays itself.

### Policy B — deterministic advertised-control sweep

Generate input sequences from GameSpec controls

## Background & Problem statement

One of the most common issues with web components is that they need to adopt the page’s existing generic styles, but can't. E.g. `<foo-button>` cannot adopt button styling unless the author duplicates button styling explicitly for it, making it hard to experiment with different components and making authors gravitate towards monolithic component libraries where they only need to define styles once.

Even outside of web components, it’s common to want to cross-reference or extend styles, e.g. `<a class="button">` is a very common pattern with `button` being in the [top 5 of most common class names](https://almanac.httparchive.org/en/2022/css#fig-5) on the Web.

Even in terms of architecture, defining default styles and then styling other structures by being able to reference these default styles would be a big win. 

Mixins somewhat mitigate but do not completely solve this problem, as they require a shared contract between the page author and the author pulling in those styles (which may be separate entities). E.g. components cannot trust that a certain mixin has been defined on the page, and even if they could, it's tedious for the page author to define new mixins every time they want to try out a new component (not to mention that as mixins are currently envisioned this would require a lot of code duplication).

`@like` is a way to target and "adopt" a set of existing CSS rules, without requiring a two-way contract. It can work with any existing stylesheet. The concept is not new, and is very similar to Sass' `@extend`. Past proposals include:
- https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/1855
- _(pretty sure there have been more)_

In this proposal I've tried to define something more concrete, and cut scope enough to avoid some of the hairiest issues while still satisfying the most prominent use cases.

## The `@like` rule

I’m envisioning a rule that would match certain rules (excluding the current rule) based on its argument and pull in their styles after applying the cascade. The target rule (the rule that contains the `@like`) would be excluded from the matching.

```css
.button {
	@like button;
}

my-input {
	@like input[type=text];
}

table.functions {
	tbody th {
		@like code;
	}
}

details.callout > summary {
	@like h4;
}
```

This may seem like too much magic, but below I discuss ways to scope it down, and make it more concrete and more implementable. However, no amount of scoping will make this a low-effort feature, the argument is just that it's also a high reward feature: **done well this has the potential to solve a multitude of very prominent author pain points**. 

## Meta-selectors

The argument to `@like` is not a selector, but a _meta-selector_: a way to describe a family of selectors. _Meta-selectors_ match any selector that consists of the same criteria regardless of specificity (and in some cases looser criteria, see below). For example, a `button` meta-selector does not just match `button`, but also `:is(button)`, `:where(button)` and so on. 

For implementation complexity to be manageable, _meta-selectors_ need to start from a very restricted set of criteria. The MVP could perhaps be a single compound selector, including:
- 0-1 type selector (or possibly even a mandatory type selector)
- 0-N action pseudo-classes (`:hover` etc)
- 0-N attribute presence and/or attribute equality selectors (`input[type=number]`). (substring matching makes it harder to determine equivalence and most use cases don't need it)
- `:not()` with a list of compound selectors matching the above (e.g. `input:not([type])`)
- Certain pseudo elements? E.g. `::placeholder`?

